# OS / editor cruft
.DS_Store
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# LaTeX build intermediates (the .tex source and final .pdf are tracked;
# these are pure regenerable byproducts of running pdflatex) -- same
# convention as the sibling PFM-STM32G474 project's .gitignore
*.aux
*.log
*.out
*.toc
*.fls
*.fdb_latexmk
*.synctex.gz

# NOTE: Debug/ build artifacts are NOT ignored here, matching the
# sibling project's convention -- if that changes, update this file.

# python/wham_console.py's own generated output -- per-shot CSV/PNG/
# JSON (shots/) and session transcripts (logs/), created on demand via
# os.makedirs(); pure operator-session data, not source, and would
# grow unbounded if tracked. See that script's own header comment.
shots/
logs/

# Core/Inc/git_version.h -- generated by python/gen_git_version.py
# (called automatically by python/wham_build.py) immediately before
# every build, baking the current commit hash + dirty-tree flag into
# *IDN?'s reply (cmd_idn(), commands.c). Deliberately NOT tracked: its
# whole job is to capture "what commit was THIS build made from," which
# is inherently redundant with git's own history for a clean build --
# a fresh clone always regenerates it correctly for HEAD, and if it
# were tracked, every single commit would spuriously modify it (the
# baked-in hash changes the instant you commit). The one case that
# genuinely isn't reconstructable from git alone -- a build made with
# UNCOMMITTED changes (dirty tree) -- isn't lost either: that exact
# state gets baked into the .elf/.bin themselves, which THIS project
# deliberately DOES track in Debug/ (see the NOTE above) -- so the
# information lives in the output artifact, not a separate source file
# that would just create merge noise. Same "regenerable byproduct,
# don't track it" reasoning as the LaTeX intermediates above; see that
# script's own header comment for the exact mechanism.
Core/Inc/git_version.h

# Core/Inc/build_target.h -- generated by python/gen_build_target.py
# (called automatically by python/wham_build.py's --target flag),
# selects CONTROLLER vs SIMULATOR firmware from this same repo, added
# 2026-09-17 for the Transrex-simulator work. Same reasoning as
# git_version.h just above: whichever target you build is a build-time
# choice, not a source-tracked one -- a fresh clone defaults to
# CONTROLLER (gen_build_target.py's own default), and tracking this
# file would make every target switch look like a spurious commit.
Core/Inc/build_target.h
